From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:15:56 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 35/44] client: the player's jump and fall as in Minecraft
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The jump rose at 6.5 m/s against a gravity of 19.6 m/s² (the setting
times the factor 2 the code calls a hack): 1.08 nodes high, at its top
after 0.33 s. Minecraft's jump is 0.42 a tick, and its air takes 0.08 a
tick off the vertical speed and then keeps 98 % of it: 1.25 high, at its
top after 0.3 s, and a fall that ends at 78.4 m/s.

The player's air is now Minecraft's, in continuous time: v -> V + (v - V)
* 0.98^(20 t), the gravity override scaling the end speed V. The jump is
the speed whose path goes through Minecraft's position at every tick
(9.28 m/s: 0.42, 0.75, 1.00, 1.17, 1.25 at ticks 1 to 5), times the jump
override. Jump held waits 10 ticks before the next jump, as Minecraft's
noJumpDelay, and the wait ends when jump is let go. movement_speed_jump
and movement_gravity no longer move the player.

Fall damage is Minecraft's: a point for each node of the fall beyond 3,
times fall_damage_add_percent, the fall's height taken from the landing
speed in that air. It was the speed over 14 m/s: nothing up to 5 nodes,
14 for 20 (Minecraft: 1 for 4, 17 for 20).

A wall pushed on in the water lifts the player at Minecraft's 0.3 a tick
as a speed of that air (6.85 m/s, it was 6), and only at the surface, as
Minecraft's jumpOutOfFluid: when the box 0.6 higher, a tick on, has no
water. It lifted along every wall under water.

Checked in the native build, keys pressed by xdotool and the position
logged every frame, at 30 and 15 FPS (Minecraft in brackets): the jump
1.26 nodes high (1.25), back on the ground after 0.54 s (0.55 to 0.6),
jump held on flat ground every 0.57 s (0.6); falls of 3, 3.5, 4, 5, 10
and 20 nodes: 0, 1, 1, 2, 7 and 17 HP (the same). Out of deep water at a
wall the feet rise 0.69 over the water's top (0.59 to 0.93), onto a slab
but not onto a whole block, as in Minecraft. The water's own numbers
(walking, swimming, sinking, the current) don't change; jump and forward
at the surface 2.36 m/s (2.33, it was 2.57).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

